course: technology
lesson: 22
title: Why planes fly
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A plane flies because its wings are shaped and tilted so that, as air rushes past, they push air downwards, and the air pushes the wing up. This upward push is called lift.
explain: Fly a kite on a windy day: its face is tilted into the wind, so it turns the rushing air downwards, and the air pushes the kite up into the sky. A wing does the same, only it is far better shaped and the plane itself races through the air.
explain: Lift only comes when air moves past the wing fast enough, so the engines push the plane forward, and a big jet must race along the runway at around 250 to 300 km/h before it can rise.
explain: High up the air is thin and freezing, so the cabin is pumped full of air, the pilots and computers watch every number, and the wings even bend and change shape on purpose.
fact: A wing pushes air downwards as the plane moves fast, and the air pushes the wing upwards; this push is called lift.
fact: A big jet must travel at around 250 to 300 km/h along the runway before it can lift off.
fact: A jet engine sucks air in with a big fan, squeezes it, burns fuel in it and blasts hot gas out of the back; in a modern airliner engine most of the push comes from the big fan at the front.
fact: Pilots fly with a stick or yoke, pedals and throttles, and read screens showing speed, height and direction; an airliner always has two pilots, and on many airlines they eat different meals so that one bad meal cannot make both ill.
fact: An autopilot is a computer that holds height, speed and direction and follows a planned route, while the pilots watch and take over; some planes can even land themselves in thick fog.
fact: Flaps and slats slide out of the wing at take-off and landing, making it bigger and more curved so the plane can fly slowly without falling.
fact: Turbulence is bumpy air, like waves in the sky; planes are built to handle much worse, and the wings bend upwards a lot in flight on purpose.
fact: Keeping your seat belt fastened whenever you are seated protects you if the plane hits sudden bumps.
fact: Airliners cruise about 10 to 12 km up, where the air is about minus 50 degrees; vapour trails are water from the engines freezing into ice crystals, and they form only when the air is cold and damp enough.
fact: The cabin is pumped full of air, but its pressure is still lower than on the ground, so ears pop as the plane climbs and descends; swallowing, yawning or sucking a sweet helps.
fact: If the cabin loses pressure, oxygen masks drop down, because in the thin air up there a person without a mask soon passes out; adults put their own mask on first, so they stay awake to help others, and the masks last long enough for the plane to descend to thicker air.
fact: A glider has no engine; it is towed up and then floats on rising warm air, and gliders can stay up for hours and fly hundreds of kilometres.
fact: Most airliners have 2 engines, and some very big ones have 4.
exercise: What makes the upward push, called lift, on a plane's wing? | The wing pushes air down as it moves, and the air pushes the wing up | The air on top must catch up with the air underneath, so the wing floats | Hot air from the engines rises and carries the plane | The plane is lighter than air once it leaves the ground
exercise: About how fast must a big jet go along the runway to lift off? | Around 250 to 300 km/h | Around 30 km/h, like a bike downhill | Around 3,000 km/h, faster than sound | It lifts off standing still once the engines roar
exercise: In a modern airliner's jet engine, where does most of the push come from? | The big fan at the front | A rocket hidden in the tail | The wheels pushing on the runway | The wings flapping a little
exercise: Why do the two pilots often eat different meals? | So a bad meal cannot make both of them ill | Because each pilot pays for their own food | So that they never feel sleepy at the same time | Because one pilot is always on a diet
exercise: If a plane has an autopilot, why does it still need pilots? | Pilots watch the computer and take over when needed | The autopilot only works at the airport | It doesn't; pilots are there only to welcome passengers | Pilots must press the autopilot button every minute
exercise: Why do flaps and slats slide out of the wing before landing? | They make the wing bigger and curvier so the plane can fly slowly | They act as brakes that scrape the runway | They catch rain so the plane is heavier | They make the plane faster for a quick landing
exercise: What is turbulence? | Bumpy air, like waves in the sky, which planes are built to handle | A sign that an engine has stopped working | A storm that always makes planes fall | The noise the wheels make on landing
exercise: When should you keep your seat belt fastened on a plane? | Whenever you are seated | Only during take-off and nothing else | Only when the pilot is shouting | Never, it is only for children
exercise: What is a vapour trail? | Water from the engine exhaust frozen into ice crystals | Smoke from fuel that has caught fire | Chemicals sprayed on purpose by the plane to change the weather below | Dust kicked up by the plane's wings
exercise: How cold is the air where airliners cruise, about 10 to 12 km up? | About minus 50 degrees | About 20 degrees, like a nice day | About 0 degrees, just freezing | Hotter than boiling, from the Sun
exercise: Why do your ears pop on take-off? | The air pressure in the cabin changes and your ears even it out | The engines are louder than ears can stand | The seat belt squeezes your head | The cold air freezes your ears
exercise: What helps your ears when they pop on a plane? | Swallowing, yawning or sucking a sweet | Holding your breath until you land | Pressing your ears hard with your fingers | Standing up and jumping
exercise: Why does an adult put on their own oxygen mask before helping a child? | So they stay awake and able to help others | Because adults are more important on a plane | Because children don't need much oxygen at all | Because the child's mask only drops down later
exercise: How can a glider stay up without an engine? | It floats on rising warm air after being towed up | It has a hidden battery in its tail | Its pilot pedals a small propeller | It is filled with a gas lighter than air, like a party balloon
exercise: Count them: 3 airliners each have 2 engines and 1 big airliner has 4. How many engines? | 10 | 9 | 6 | 12
test: «my dad said planes fly coz the air on top has to race to meet the air underneath» What is a better explanation? | The tilted wing pushes air down, and the air pushes the wing up | Your dad is right, the air on top must race to meet the air underneath at the back | Planes fly because they are lighter than air | Planes fly because the engines point upwards
test: «bloody turbulence again, we're gonna fall out the sky» What is right? | Planes are built for much worse; just keep your seat belt on | Yes, a big bump can make any plane fall | Only if the wings bend, which means they are breaking off and the plane will drop | Turbulence only happens when the pilot is careless
test: You look out of the window at landing and the wing seems to grow and bend at the back. What is happening? | Flaps and slats are sliding out so the plane can fly slowly | The wing is breaking because of the cold | The wing is folding up to fit the gate | The fuel is leaking out of the wing, so the wing shrinks and bends
test: «why did that plane leave a white line and the other one didn't» What is right? | Trails form only when the air up there is cold and damp enough | Only one of them was spraying chemicals | One plane was burning fuel, the other was gliding along without engines | The second plane had its engines switched off
test: What would happen if a big jet went along the runway at only 50 km/h? | It would not get enough lift and would stay on the ground | It would lift off slowly and gently, like a big kite in the wind | It would fly, but only very low | It would bounce up and down
test: Nan's ears hurt as the plane comes down to land. What can she do? | Swallow, yawn or suck a sweet | Hold her breath for ten minutes | Put her hands over her ears tightly | Nothing at all, sore ears can't be helped
test: In a film, a man takes off his oxygen mask high up to chat and feels fine for an hour. What is wrong? | Without the mask in thin air at that height he would soon pass out | Nothing, the air up there is just like the air on the ground, only colder | Oxygen masks are only for decoration | Planes always carry fresh air from the ground
test: «autopilot does it all, pilots just nap lol» What is right? | No, pilots watch the computers and take over when needed | Yes, pilots sleep on every flight | Yes, the autopilot even talks to the passengers and brings the drinks round | No, autopilot is only switched on at the gate
test: Which is true about a glider? | It has no engine and can fly for hours on rising warm air | It must land within a minute of being let go by the tow plane | It flies only because of the wind behind it | It is pushed by a small jet engine in the nose
test: Mia counts the wings: 4 toy planes on the shelf, each with 2 main wings. How many main wings? | 8 | 6 | 4 | 10
test: «a jet engine is basically just a big hairdryer innit» What is a better description? | It sucks air in with a big fan, burns fuel in it and blasts gas out of the back | Yes, it only blows warm air | It is a giant propeller with no fuel | It pulls the plane by sucking the air in front
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of the lift sustaining an aircraft in flight? | The inclined wing deflects air downwards, whereupon the air exerts an upward force upon the wing | The air above the wing is obliged to rejoin the air beneath it at the trailing edge | The aircraft becomes lighter than air upon departing the ground | The heat of the engines causes the aircraft to ascend
exam: Identify the principal reason for which flaps and slats are deployed prior to landing. | They enlarge and curve the wing, permitting flight at a reduced speed | They are intended to abrade the runway and arrest the aircraft | They serve to collect precipitation and increase the mass of the aircraft | They are deployed solely in order to increase velocity
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning vapour trails. | They consist of water vapour from the engine exhaust which has frozen into ice crystals in cold, humid air | They consist of chemical substances dispersed deliberately in order to modify the weather | They consist of smoke from fuel which has caught fire | They consist of dust raised from the surface of the wings
exam: It is given that the cabin pressure has failed. Ascertain the reason for which an adult is instructed to fit his or her own mask before assisting a child. | The adult thereby remains conscious and capable of rendering assistance | Adult passengers are accorded priority under all regulations | Children are not affected by a reduction in pressure | The child's mask is released at a subsequent stage
